A constraint decoded nothing, so every constraint in every nib was empty.

-[NSLayoutConstraint initWithCoder:] set a priority and returned. No items, no attributes, no
relation, no constant, and a multiplier left at the zero from alloc. The layout trace says exactly
what that produced, 58 times over in one window:

  cider-layout   all: nil 0x0 .none == nil 0x0 .none * 0 + 0 active=1 prio=1000

THE SOLVER WAS NEVER THE PROBLEM. It runs, it is handed the constraints, and there is nothing in
them to solve. That is worth saying because the previous patch left "a solver able to satisfy these
constraints" as the next link, and the measurement says the link was one step earlier.

THE KEYS ARE READ OUT OF THE ARCHIVE, not guessed. Every one of the 58 NSLayoutConstraint objects
in iA Writer GeneralPreferences.nib carries NSFirstItem, NSSecondItem and both attributes in two
spellings; 34 carry a constant, 15 a relation, 1 a priority. NOTHING carries a multiplier, so it
defaults to 1: the zero from alloc would otherwise cancel every relation that has a second item.
The V2 spelling wins where both are present. Items are stored WITHOUT retaining, as the designated
initialiser does, because a constraint that retained its views would keep the whole tree alive.

After it, the same 60 constraints read as real rules:

  NSView .trailing  == NSBox .trailing * 1 + 20
  NSBox .leading    == NSView .leading * 1 + 20
  NSBox .top        == NSStackView .bottom * 1 + 15
  NSTextField .leading >= NSView .leading * 1 + 20

Zero of them are empty where all 58 were before.

THIS MAKES ONE WINDOW WORSE AND I AM NOT GOING TO CALL IT A FIX. iA Writer Preferences gains the
full text of its two explanatory paragraphs, which were truncated, and LOSES its Light and Dark
radio buttons and its Get Ready-Made Shortcuts button, which are no longer drawn at all. The
Appearance and Dock icon labels still overlap. The solver now acts on real rules and places some
views off the pane or at no size, which the stale Interface Builder frames were accidentally
hiding.

It is landed anyway because an empty constraint is never correct, because nothing can be fixed
above this while the inputs are blank, and because the blast radius is measured rather than
assumed: the roster is BYTE IDENTICAL after it. The reason is worth recording. Constraints built in
CODE were always populated, so iA Writer 212 constraint document window never depended on this, and
the roster gated paths load no nib that carries constraints. Only nib decoded constraints were
empty, which is why this hid.

NEXT: the solver, with real inputs for the first time. A view that vanishes is being given a frame
off the pane or of zero size, and CIDER_TRACE_LAYOUT prints every rule it applies.

